        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/ec9ddb3b-2da7-4fc0-9e45-923e0150a8c1,1772677104353/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C573%2C5500%2C3093%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='Smoke is seen over a road in the Middle East.' width='620' height='349' title='FILE PHOTO: Smoke rises after reported Iranian missile attacks, following United States and Israel strikes on Iran, as seen from Doha, Qatar, March 1, 2026. REUTERS/Mohammed Salem/File Photo'/><p>Confusing advice and on-again-off-again airport closures are complicating the evacuation of hundreds of thousands of foreigners stranded in the conflict zone around the Persian Gulf. In a region that has seen tragic mistakes resulting in the deaths of hundreds of civilian air passengers, decisions on whether to fly remain fairly ad hoc and can come down to the judgment of an individual pilot. \n</p>",
