        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/cba8046c-3f88-4331-bfb7-fbce7c3b0f4c,1772650752759/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C447%2C4240%2C2385%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A middle-aged couple stand in a yard strewn with renovation material.' width='620' height='349' title='Christine and Dan Reio stand by their property in Wainfleet, Ont., in November 2025. The couple may have to pay hundreds of thousands of dollars for a provincially mandated archeological investigation after Indigenous ancestral remains were discovered during home renovations.'/><p>An Ontario couple was concerned to discover Indigenous ancestral remains on their property during renovations. But the biggest shock came when they learned a provincially mandated investigation could cost $319,000. Experts say the remains must be protected and treated with dignity \u2014 but that the current law should be updated so situations like this don't happen.\n</p>",
